import{t as e}from"./index-Dmb4eRxh.js";var t={meta:e,chapters:JSON.parse(`[{"number":1,"title":"","summary":"At dawn in his brush hut by the sea, the pearl diver Kino feels content, until a scorpion stings his baby son, Coyotito. The family begs the town doctor for help, but he refuses to treat a poor native family.","details":["Kino wakes before dawn in his brush house near the beach, beside his wife, Juana, and their baby, Coyotito, who sleeps in a hanging box. In his head he hears music his people have passed down: a calm, whole Song of the Family. Juana makes corn cakes and the morning feels complete.","Then a scorpion creeps down the rope of Coyotito’s box. Kino hears a darker music, the Song of Evil, and moves slowly to catch it, but the baby laughs and shakes the rope, and the scorpion falls and stings him on the shoulder. Kino crushes the scorpion. Juana sucks out what poison she can, the neighbours, among them Kino’s older brother Juan Tomás and his wife Apolonia, crowd into the doorway, and Juana demands the doctor, who has never come to their poor quarter.","The family walks into town, followed by curious neighbours and the beggars from the church steps. The doctor belongs to the race that conquered Kino’s people centuries ago, Kino addresses the doctor’s servant, one of his own people, in their old language, but the servant will not answer in it. Told that Kino has only a few small, misshapen pearls to pay with, the doctor sends word that he is out. Humiliated, Kino strikes the gate with his fist and cuts his knuckles open."],"characters":["kino","juana","coyotito","the-scorpion","the-neighbors","juan-tomas","apolonia","the-beggars","the-doctor","the-doctors-servant","kinos-house"]},{"number":2,"title":"","summary":"Kino dives for oysters while Juana treats Coyotito with a seaweed poultice and prays for luck. Kino finds a huge oyster, and inside it the greatest pearl anyone has ever seen.","details":["The family goes down to the shore and Kino’s canoe, his most valuable possession, handed down from his grandfather. Juana makes a poultice of brown seaweed for Coyotito’s swollen shoulder, a remedy that may be as good as the doctor’s, though no one values it because it costs nothing.","Out over the oyster bed, Kino dives with a stone and a basket, hearing the Song of the Pearl That Might Be. Juana prays, not quite out loud, that he will find a pearl to pay the doctor. Near the end of his dive he spots one large oyster lying apart from the rest and brings it up.","When he pries open the big oyster, he finds a pearl the size of a gull’s egg, perfectly shaped and glowing: the Pearl of the World. Juana sees that the swelling on Coyotito’s shoulder is going down. Kino throws back his head and howls, and the other canoes race toward him."],"characters":["kino","juana","coyotito","the-pearl","kinos-canoe","the-gulf"]},{"number":3,"title":"","summary":"News of the pearl stirs up the whole town. Kino dreams aloud of a church wedding, new clothes, a rifle and schooling for Coyotito. The doctor suddenly comes to treat the baby, and that night someone breaks in to steal the pearl.","details":["Before Kino is even home, the town knows about the pearl, and everyone starts to want something from it: the priest thinks of church repairs, the shopkeepers of goods to sell, the beggars of alms, the pearl buyers of profit. The doctor tells a patient that the baby with the scorpion sting is his client. Kino has become everyone’s enemy without knowing it.","In his hut, surrounded by neighbours and his brother, Juan Tomás, Kino says what the pearl will buy: a proper church wedding with Juana, new clothes, a rifle, and above all school for Coyotito, so he can read and know things. The neighbours are startled by his boldness. The priest visits to remind Kino to give thanks for his treasure and to pray for guidance.","The doctor arrives with his servant, claims scorpion poison can strike later, and gives Coyotito a white powder. Within the hour the baby falls violently ill, and the doctor returns to “cure” him; Kino suspects a trick. When the doctor asks about the pearl, Kino’s glance gives away where it is hidden, so afterwards he digs it up and hides it somewhere new.","That night someone creeps into the hut. Kino fights the intruder in the dark, and is struck on the head as the attacker escapes. Juana begs him to destroy the pearl or throw it back into the sea, saying it will destroy them. Kino refuses: this is their one chance, and he means to fight for it."],"characters":["kino","juana","coyotito","the-pearl","the-priest","the-beggars","the-pearl-buyers","the-doctor","the-doctors-servant","juan-tomas","the-neighbors","the-night-attackers","kinos-house","the-gulf"]},{"number":4,"title":"","summary":"Kino takes the pearl to the buyers, who pretend to compete but offer him a fraction of its worth. He refuses and decides to sell in the capital. Juan Tomás warns him of the danger, and that night Kino is attacked again.","details":["The whole neighbourhood follows Kino, Juana and Coyotito to the pearl buyers’ street. The buyers appear to be rivals, but the town suspects they all work for a single man. The first buyer, who rolls a coin across his knuckles as he talks, examines the pearl and dismisses it as a curiosity too large to be valuable, offering a thousand pesos.","Kino is outraged: he knows it is worth fifty thousand. The other buyers are called in one by one, and each disparages the pearl and offers less or nothing; the first buyer then raises his offer to fifteen hundred. Sensing a trap, Kino snatches it back and announces that he will go to the capital to sell it himself.","That evening Juan Tomás reminds him of men who once tried to sell their pearls in the capital and were never heard of again. Kino has defied the whole system of the town, and he is afraid, but determined. That night, outside the hut, he is attacked in the dark again. He slashes out with his knife and drives the attackers off, but is left bleeding. Juana begs him again to give up the pearl; Kino insists that he is a man and will go to the capital."],"characters":["kino","juana","coyotito","the-pearl","the-neighbors","the-pearl-buyers","juan-tomas","the-night-attackers","the-capital"]},{"number":5,"title":"","summary":"Juana tries to throw the pearl into the sea and Kino beats her. Attacked once more, Kino kills a man. His canoe is smashed and his house burned, and the family hides with Juan Tomás before fleeing north.","details":["In the night Juana takes the pearl and slips down to the shore to throw it into the sea. Kino catches her, tears it from her hand, strikes her in the face and kicks her. She watches him without anger: she understands that the pearl has driven him half mad.","Walking back, Kino is attacked by several men. Juana, recovering, finds the pearl lying on the path, and then sees Kino struggling to his feet beside a dead man with a knife wound in his throat. Kino has killed someone, and they must flee.","Juana runs for Coyotito while Kino goes to prepare the canoe, but someone has smashed a hole in its bottom: a crime worse than killing a man, Kino feels. Then the hut bursts into flames. With nowhere else to go, the family hides in Juan Tomás’s house, where his wife, Apolonia, is mourning them as dead. Juan Tomás keeps them hidden all day and gathers supplies.","Juan Tomás asks whether Kino should get rid of the pearl, but Kino says the pearl has become his soul. When darkness falls, Kino, Juana and Coyotito set out north toward the capital."],"characters":["kino","juana","coyotito","the-pearl","the-night-attackers","juan-tomas","apolonia","the-neighbors","kinos-house","kinos-canoe","the-capital","the-gulf"]},{"number":6,"title":"","summary":"Fleeing north, the family is hunted by trackers. In the mountains Kino attacks them at night, but a rifle shot kills Coyotito. Kino and Juana walk back to town, and Kino throws the pearl into the sea.","details":["They travel by night and hide by day. Kino finds himself hoping again, picturing the future the pearl will bring. But when he looks back from a hiding place, he sees trackers: two men on foot reading the ground, and a horseman with a rifle, following their trail.","Kino tries to hide their tracks and they climb into the mountains, toward a pool of water among high rocks with small caves above it. Juana and Coyotito hide in a cave, but the trackers come and make camp beside the pool. Kino decides he must attack them before dawn, while the rifleman dozes.","He strips off his white clothes so they won’t show in the dark and creeps down. Just as he is ready, Coyotito cries out in the cave. One tracker thinks it is a coyote pup, and the rifleman fires toward the sound. Kino leaps on them and kills all three men, seizing the rifle. Then he hears Juana’s keening from the cave: the shot has killed Coyotito.","Late the next afternoon, Kino and Juana walk back into the town side by side, Kino carrying the rifle and Juana carrying the bundle of her dead child in her shawl. People step aside in silence. At the water’s edge, Kino offers the pearl to Juana, but she will not take it and leaves it to him. He throws it as far as he can, and it sinks to the bottom of the sea."],"characters":["kino","juana","coyotito","the-pearl","the-trackers","the-gulf","the-mountain-pool"]}]`),characters:JSON.parse(`[{"id":"apolonia","name":"Apolonia","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"Kino’s family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Juan Tomás’s wife."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Juan Tomás’s wife, who crowds into Kino’s doorway with her children after the scorpion sting."},{"from":5,"text":"When Kino’s house burns, she mourns his family as dead, until they turn up at her door. She helps keep them hidden."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"coyotito","name":"Coyotito","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"Kino’s family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino and Juana’s baby son, stung by a scorpion."},{"from":3,"text":"The baby whose future Kino dreams the pearl will buy."},{"from":6,"text":"Kino and Juana’s son, killed by a tracker’s shot."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino and Juana’s infant son, who sleeps in a hanging box. A scorpion stings him on the shoulder, and the doctor refuses to treat him."},{"from":2,"text":"The swelling begins to go down after Juana’s poultice."},{"from":3,"text":"Kino dreams of sending him to school so he can read and be free. After the doctor gives him a white powder, he falls sick, and the doctor then “cures” him."},{"from":6,"text":"While hiding in a cave, he cries out; a tracker mistakes the sound for a coyote and fires, killing him."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Was stung by a scorpion."},{"chapter":3,"text":"Fell ill after the doctor’s powder."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Was killed by a rifle shot fired toward his cry."}]},{"id":"juan-tomas","name":"Juan Tomás","firstChapter":1,"importance":"supporting","group":"Kino’s family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino’s older brother."},{"from":4,"text":"Kino’s brother, a cautious adviser."},{"from":5,"text":"Kino’s brother, who hides the family when they must flee."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino’s older brother, who crowds into Kino’s doorway with his family after the scorpion stings Coyotito."},{"from":3,"text":"He stands beside Kino as the neighbours gather to hear what the pearl will buy."},{"from":4,"text":"Wiser in the ways of the world, he warns Kino that men who have gone to sell pearls in the capital have disappeared, and that he is defying the whole way of life in the town. Still, he supports him."},{"from":5,"text":"He risks his own safety to hide Kino’s family for a day, gathers supplies for them and helps them escape at night."}],"moments":[{"chapter":4,"text":"Warned Kino of the dangers of going to the capital."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Hid Kino’s family in his house."}]},{"id":"juana","name":"Juana","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"Kino’s family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino’s strong, devoted wife."},{"from":3,"text":"Kino’s wife, who fears the pearl is evil."},{"from":6,"text":"A mother in mourning, who will not take the pearl back."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino’s wife and Coyotito’s mother. Patient and practical, she acts fast when the scorpion strikes, sucking the poison from the wound and insisting they go to the doctor. Kino marvels at her strength."},{"from":2,"text":"She treats Coyotito with a seaweed poultice and prays for Kino to find a pearl."},{"from":3,"text":"After the night attack she begs Kino to destroy the pearl, convinced it is evil and will destroy their family."},{"from":4,"text":"She stands by Kino’s decision, though she is afraid."},{"from":5,"text":"She tries to throw the pearl into the sea and is beaten for it, but does not turn against Kino. When he kills a man, she decides that they must flee together."},{"from":6,"text":"She hides with Coyotito in a cave while Kino attacks the trackers. After Coyotito is killed, she walks back to town beside Kino, carrying her son, and refuses the pearl when Kino offers it to her."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Sucked the scorpion’s poison from Coyotito’s shoulder."},{"chapter":2,"text":"Treated Coyotito with a seaweed poultice."},{"chapter":3,"text":"Begged Kino to throw the pearl away."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Tried to throw the pearl into the sea."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Refused the pearl when Kino offered it to her."}]},{"id":"kino","name":"Kino","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"Kino’s family","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"A poor native pearl diver, content with his family."},{"from":2,"text":"The diver who finds the Pearl of the World."},{"from":3,"text":"A diver whose pearl brings dreams, and danger."},{"from":4,"text":"A man defying the pearl buyers and the town."},{"from":5,"text":"A man on the run after killing an attacker."},{"from":6,"text":"A grieving father who gives the pearl back to the sea."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"A young pearl diver who lives with his wife, Juana, and baby son, Coyotito, in a brush hut by the sea. He hears his people’s old songs in his head: the Song of the Family when all is well, the Song of Evil when danger comes. When the doctor refuses to treat his son, his helpless anger at the people who have long looked down on his race boils over."},{"from":2,"text":"A skilled diver who owns little except his canoe, inherited from his grandfather. He finds an enormous, perfect pearl."},{"from":3,"text":"The pearl awakens his ambitions: a church wedding, new clothes, a rifle and, most of all, an education for Coyotito. He grows suspicious of everyone. Attacked in the night, he refuses Juana’s plea to throw the pearl away, insisting it is their one chance."},{"from":4,"text":"He refuses the buyers’ insulting offers and resolves to go to the capital, defying a system that has always kept his people poor. He is frightened, but stubborn and proud."},{"from":5,"text":"Obsessed with the pearl, he beats Juana when she tries to throw it away, then kills a man who attacks him. With his canoe destroyed and his house burned, he flees north with his family, saying the pearl has become his soul."},{"from":6,"text":"Hunted by trackers, he kills all three, but a rifle shot kills Coyotito. He returns to town with Juana and throws the pearl into the sea."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Struck the doctor’s gate after being turned away."},{"chapter":2,"text":"Found the Pearl of the World."},{"chapter":3,"text":"Told his neighbours what the pearl would buy."},{"chapter":3,"text":"Fought off an intruder in the night."},{"chapter":4,"text":"Refused the pearl buyers’ offers and decided to go to the capital."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Beat Juana when she tried to throw the pearl away."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Killed one of the men who attacked him."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Killed the three trackers."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Threw the pearl back into the sea."}]},{"id":"kinos-house","kind":"place","name":"Kino’s brush house","firstChapter":1,"importance":"supporting","group":"Places","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"The family’s small brush hut near the beach."},{"from":5,"text":"The family’s hut, burned to the ground."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"A hut of brush near the shore, where Kino, Juana and Coyotito live among their neighbours. Coyotito sleeps in a box hanging from the roof."},{"from":3,"text":"Kino hides the pearl in a hole in its floor. In the night an intruder creeps in to search for it."},{"from":5,"text":"It is set on fire and burns down, leaving the family with nowhere to go."}],"moments":[{"chapter":3,"text":"Was searched by an intruder in the night."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Was burned to the ground."}]},{"id":"the-beggars","name":"The beggars","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"The town","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Beggars outside the church who know everyone’s business."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"They sit on the church steps and know all about the doctor’s greed and his past. They follow Kino to the doctor’s house to watch."},{"from":3,"text":"They are glad to hear about the pearl, hoping Kino will give alms."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"the-doctor","name":"The doctor","firstChapter":1,"importance":"major","group":"The town","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"A greedy, self-indulgent doctor who refuses to treat poor natives."},{"from":3,"text":"The doctor who suddenly wants to treat Coyotito, now that there is a pearl."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"A fat, lazy man from the race that conquered Kino’s people, who longs for his past life in Paris. He refuses to treat Coyotito when Kino can pay only with a few worthless pearls."},{"from":3,"text":"When he hears of the pearl, he arrives uninvited, warns that scorpion poison can strike later, and gives Coyotito a powder; the baby falls ill and the doctor returns to “cure” him. Kino suspects him of making the child sick on purpose, and he tries to find out where the pearl is hidden."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Refused to treat Coyotito."},{"chapter":3,"text":"Gave Coyotito a powder, then returned to “cure” him."}]},{"id":"the-doctors-servant","name":"The doctor’s servant","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"The town","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"The doctor’s servant, one of Kino’s own people."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"He belongs to Kino’s race, but refuses to speak their old language at the doctor’s door and turns the family away with the doctor’s lie that he is out."},{"from":3,"text":"He accompanies the doctor when the doctor comes uninvited to treat Coyotito."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"the-neighbors","name":"The neighbours","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"The town","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"Kino’s neighbours in the brush houses, always watching."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"They follow Kino’s family to the doctor’s house, curious to see what happens."},{"from":3,"text":"They crowd into Kino’s hut to hear his plans and are startled by his boldness."},{"from":4,"text":"They walk with Kino to the pearl buyers, and afterwards argue over whether he was right to refuse."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"the-scorpion","name":"The scorpion","firstChapter":1,"importance":"minor","group":"Danger","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":1,"text":"The scorpion that stings Coyotito."}],"description":[{"from":1,"text":"It creeps down the rope of Coyotito’s box and stings him when he laughs and shakes the rope. Kino crushes it."}],"moments":[{"chapter":1,"text":"Stung Coyotito."}]},{"id":"kinos-canoe","kind":"object","name":"Kino’s canoe","firstChapter":2,"importance":"supporting","group":"Objects","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":2,"text":"Kino’s canoe, his most valuable possession."},{"from":5,"text":"Kino’s canoe, smashed by an unknown enemy."}],"description":[{"from":2,"text":"An old canoe handed down from Kino’s grandfather, which he uses to dive for oysters. It is the one thing of value he owns, the means of feeding his family."},{"from":5,"text":"When Kino goes to prepare it for their escape, he finds a hole smashed in its bottom. To him this is a worse crime than killing a man, because a canoe is a man’s livelihood."}],"moments":[{"chapter":2,"text":"Carried Kino out to the oyster bed where he found the pearl."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Was found with a hole smashed in its bottom."}]},{"id":"the-gulf","kind":"place","name":"The Gulf","firstChapter":2,"importance":"supporting","group":"Places","aliases":[{"name":"the sea","from":2}],"tagline":[{"from":2,"text":"The sea where Kino dives for oysters."},{"from":6,"text":"The sea that takes the pearl back."}],"description":[{"from":2,"text":"The calm waters off the beach, with an oyster bed where the pearl divers work. Kino finds the great pearl there."},{"from":3,"text":"Juana wants to throw the pearl back into it."},{"from":5,"text":"Juana slips down to the shore at night to throw the pearl into it, but Kino catches her and tears it from her hand."},{"from":6,"text":"Kino throws the pearl far out into the water, and it sinks to the bottom."}],"moments":[{"chapter":2,"text":"Gave up the great pearl from its oyster bed."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Juana nearly threw the pearl into it."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Received the pearl back."}]},{"id":"the-pearl","name":"The Pearl of the World","firstChapter":2,"importance":"major","group":"Objects","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":2,"text":"The greatest pearl ever found."},{"from":3,"text":"A treasure that awakens greed all over town."},{"from":5,"text":"A treasure that has cost a life and a home."},{"from":6,"text":"Returned to the bottom of the sea."}],"description":[{"from":2,"text":"A pearl the size of a gull’s egg, perfectly round and glowing, found by Kino in a huge oyster."},{"from":3,"text":"Everyone in town begins to scheme over it. To Kino it shows pictures of a better future; to Juana it seems evil."},{"from":4,"text":"The buyers call it too big to be valuable, a mere curiosity, but Kino believes it is worth fifty thousand pesos."},{"from":5,"text":"Kino says it has become his soul; to keep it he has lost his canoe and his house."},{"from":6,"text":"Looking into it after Coyotito’s death, Kino sees only ugliness. He throws it back into the sea."}],"moments":[{"chapter":2,"text":"Was found by Kino."},{"chapter":4,"text":"Was appraised at a fraction of its worth by the buyers."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Was thrown back into the sea."}],"kind":"object"},{"id":"the-night-attackers","name":"The attackers in the dark","firstChapter":3,"importance":"supporting","group":"Danger","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":3,"text":"An unseen intruder who comes in the dark for the pearl."},{"from":5,"text":"Unknown enemies who have cost Kino his canoe and home."}],"description":[{"from":3,"text":"Someone creeps into Kino’s hut in the dark and strikes him before escaping. Kino cannot see who it is."},{"from":4,"text":"Kino is attacked again outside his hut, this time by more than one figure."},{"from":5,"text":"Several men attack Kino on the path; he kills one. Someone smashes his canoe and burns his house. Who sent them remains unknown."}],"moments":[{"chapter":3,"text":"Broke into Kino’s hut."},{"chapter":4,"text":"Attacked Kino outside his hut."},{"chapter":5,"text":"Attacked Kino on the path; one was killed."}]},{"id":"the-pearl-buyers","name":"The pearl buyers","firstChapter":3,"importance":"supporting","group":"The pearl trade","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":3,"text":"The town’s pearl dealers, eager for Kino’s pearl."},{"from":4,"text":"Dealers who pretend to compete, but try to cheat Kino together."}],"description":[{"from":3,"text":"When they hear of the pearl, they plan how to get it as cheaply as possible."},{"from":4,"text":"They seem to be rivals, but the town believes they all work for one man. The first, who rolls a coin across his knuckles, calls the pearl a worthless curiosity and offers a thousand pesos; the rest offer even less."}],"moments":[{"chapter":4,"text":"Offered Kino a fraction of the pearl’s worth."}]},{"id":"the-priest","name":"The priest","firstChapter":3,"importance":"supporting","group":"The town","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":3,"text":"The town priest, who hopes the pearl will pay for church repairs."}],"description":[{"from":3,"text":"When he hears about the pearl he thinks of repairs the church needs, then visits Kino to remind him to give thanks to God and to point out that he and Juana were never married in the church."}],"moments":[{"chapter":3,"text":"Visited Kino after hearing about the pearl."}]},{"id":"the-capital","kind":"place","name":"The capital","firstChapter":4,"importance":"minor","group":"Places","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":4,"text":"The distant city where Kino hopes to sell his pearl."}],"description":[{"from":4,"text":"After the town’s buyers try to cheat him, Kino decides to take the pearl to the capital and sell it there. Juan Tomás reminds him of men who set out to sell pearls there and never returned."},{"from":5,"text":"The family flees north toward it."}],"moments":[]},{"id":"the-mountain-pool","kind":"place","name":"The mountain pool","firstChapter":6,"importance":"minor","group":"Places","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"A pool of water high in the rocks, with caves above it."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"A small pool fed by a spring, high in the mountains, where animals and travellers come to drink. Juana and Coyotito hide in a cave above it, and the trackers make camp beside it."}],"moments":[{"chapter":6,"text":"Was where the trackers made camp, below the family’s hiding place."}]},{"id":"the-trackers","name":"The trackers","firstChapter":6,"importance":"supporting","group":"Danger","aliases":[],"tagline":[{"from":6,"text":"Two trackers and a rifleman hunting Kino’s family."}],"description":[{"from":6,"text":"Two men on foot who read the ground for signs, and a horseman with a rifle. They follow Kino’s family into the mountains and camp by the pool below their hiding place. When Coyotito cries, the rifleman fires at the sound. Kino kills all three."}],"moments":[{"chapter":6,"text":"Fired at Coyotito’s cry, killing him."},{"chapter":6,"text":"Were killed by Kino."}]}]`),relationships:JSON.parse(`[{"a":"kino","b":"juana","states":[{"from":1,"type":"love","label":"Devoted husband and wife","kin":"spouse"},{"from":3,"type":"love","label":"Married partners divided over the pearl","kin":"spouse"},{"from":5,"type":"love","label":"Kino beats Juana for trying to throw the pearl away; she stays loyal","kin":"spouse"},{"from":6,"type":"love","label":"Return to town side by side, united in grief","kin":"spouse"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"coyotito","states":[{"from":1,"type":"family","label":"Father and baby son","kin":"parent"},{"from":3,"type":"family","label":"Kino dreams of schooling for his son","kin":"parent"},{"from":6,"type":"family","label":"Kino’s son, killed while Kino attacks the trackers","kin":"parent"}]},{"a":"juana","b":"coyotito","states":[{"from":1,"type":"family","label":"Mother and baby son","kin":"parent"},{"from":6,"type":"family","label":"Juana carries her dead son home","kin":"parent"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"juan-tomas","states":[{"from":1,"type":"family","label":"Brothers","kin":"sibling"},{"from":4,"type":"family","label":"Brother who warns Kino of the danger","kin":"sibling"},{"from":5,"type":"family","label":"Brother who hides Kino’s family","kin":"sibling"}]},{"a":"juan-tomas","b":"apolonia","states":[{"from":1,"type":"love","label":"Husband and wife","kin":"spouse"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"apolonia","states":[{"from":1,"type":"family","label":"Sister-in-law","kin":"relative"},{"from":5,"type":"family","label":"Sister-in-law who helps hide the family","kin":"relative"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-doctor","states":[{"from":1,"type":"enemy","label":"Refuses to treat Kino’s son"},{"from":3,"type":"enemy","label":"Kino suspects him of poisoning Coyotito for the pearl"}]},{"a":"the-doctor","b":"coyotito","states":[{"from":1,"type":"enemy","label":"Refuses to treat the stung baby"},{"from":3,"type":"enemy","label":"Gives him a powder that makes him sick"}]},{"a":"the-doctor","b":"the-doctors-servant","states":[{"from":1,"type":"work","label":"Master and servant"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-doctors-servant","states":[{"from":1,"type":"enemy","label":"Turns Kino away at the gate"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-priest","states":[{"from":3,"type":"work","label":"Priest who reminds Kino of his duties, and the church’s needs"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-pearl-buyers","states":[{"from":3,"type":"work","label":"Dealers who will set the pearl’s price"},{"from":4,"type":"enemy","label":"Try to cheat Kino with low offers"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-night-attackers","states":[{"from":3,"type":"enemy","label":"Breaks in to steal the pearl"},{"from":4,"type":"enemy","label":"Attack Kino again outside his hut"},{"from":5,"type":"enemy","label":"Kino kills one; they smash his canoe and burn his house"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-trackers","states":[{"from":6,"type":"enemy","label":"Hunt Kino’s family; Kino kills them"}]},{"a":"the-trackers","b":"coyotito","states":[{"from":6,"type":"enemy","label":"A tracker’s shot kills Coyotito"}]},{"a":"coyotito","b":"the-scorpion","states":[{"from":1,"type":"enemy","label":"Stings the baby"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-neighbors","states":[{"from":1,"type":"friend","label":"Follow Kino’s family to the doctor"},{"from":3,"type":"friend","label":"Gather to hear Kino’s dreams"}]},{"a":"the-beggars","b":"the-doctor","states":[{"from":1,"type":"work","label":"Beggars who know all about the doctor’s greed"}]},{"a":"the-beggars","b":"kino","states":[{"from":1,"type":"friend","label":"Follow Kino to the doctor’s door"},{"from":3,"type":"friend","label":"Hope Kino will give alms"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":2,"type":"tie","label":"Kino’s great find and hope"},{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Kino’s dreams depend on it"},{"from":5,"type":"tie","label":"Kino would kill to keep it; calls it his soul"},{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"Kino throws it back into the sea"}]},{"a":"juana","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":2,"type":"tie","label":"An answer to Juana’s prayer"},{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Juana believes it is evil"},{"from":5,"type":"tie","label":"She tries to throw it into the sea"},{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"She refuses it when Kino offers it"}]},{"a":"the-doctor","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Tries to learn where it is hidden"}]},{"a":"the-priest","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Hopes it will pay for church repairs"}]},{"a":"the-pearl-buyers","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Plan to get it cheaply"},{"from":4,"type":"tie","label":"Call it a worthless curiosity"}]},{"a":"the-night-attackers","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Try to steal it"}]},{"a":"the-beggars","b":"the-pearl","states":[{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"Hope it will bring them alms"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"kinos-canoe","states":[{"from":2,"type":"tie","label":"Kino’s inherited canoe and livelihood"},{"from":5,"type":"tie","label":"Kino finds it smashed"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"kinos-house","states":[{"from":1,"type":"tie","label":"Kino’s family home"},{"from":5,"type":"tie","label":"Burned down"}]},{"a":"juana","b":"kinos-house","states":[{"from":1,"type":"tie","label":"Juana’s home"}]},{"a":"coyotito","b":"kinos-house","states":[{"from":1,"type":"tie","label":"Stung in his hanging box here"}]},{"a":"the-night-attackers","b":"kinos-house","states":[{"from":3,"type":"tie","label":"An intruder searches it at night"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-gulf","states":[{"from":2,"type":"tie","label":"Dives its oyster bed for a living"},{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"Throws the pearl back into it"}]},{"a":"the-pearl","b":"the-gulf","states":[{"from":2,"type":"tie","label":"Found in its oyster bed"},{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"Sinks back to its bottom"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-capital","states":[{"from":4,"type":"tie","label":"Where Kino plans to sell the pearl"},{"from":5,"type":"tie","label":"Where the family is fleeing"}]},{"a":"juan-tomas","b":"the-capital","states":[{"from":4,"type":"tie","label":"Warns of sellers who vanished on the way"}]},{"a":"the-trackers","b":"the-mountain-pool","states":[{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"Camp beside it"}]},{"a":"juana","b":"the-mountain-pool","states":[{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"Hides with Coyotito in a cave above it"}]},{"a":"kino","b":"the-mountain-pool","states":[{"from":6,"type":"tie","label":"Creeps down to attack the trackers"}]}]`),notes:[{id:`parable`,term:`parable`,kind:`reference`,chapter:1,text:`A simple story told to teach a lesson. Steinbeck heard a version of this tale, a local folk story, in La Paz during a 1940 marine-biology voyage, and his opening invites readers to find their own meaning in it.`},{id:`la-paz`,term:`La Paz`,kind:`place`,chapter:1,text:`The unnamed town is modelled on La Paz, near the southern tip of Mexico's Baja California peninsula, on the Gulf of California. In the 1940s it was a small, remote port long famous for its pearls.`,placeId:`the-town`},{id:`the-songs`,term:`the Songs`,kind:`reference`,chapter:1,text:`Steinbeck's own device: Kino's people once made songs for everything, and he still hears them in his head. The Song of the Family means wholeness and safety; the Song of Evil, or of the Enemy, signals danger. Listen for which song is playing.`,seeAlso:[2,3,4,6]},{id:`brush-house`,term:`brush house`,kind:`custom`,chapter:1,text:`A hut with walls of woven sticks and brush under a thatched roof: the home of the poorest fishing families, who live on the beach at the edge of the stone-and-plaster town.`,placeId:`kinos-house`},{id:`corncakes-and-pulque`,term:`corncakes and pulque`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:1,text:`The corncakes are tortillas, the everyday flatbread of Mexico, cooked on a clay griddle. Pulque is a milky, mildly alcoholic drink made from fermented agave sap, brewed since long before the Spanish arrived.`},{id:`coyotito`,term:`Coyotito`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:1,text:`Spanish: 'little coyote', a diminutive and affectionate name. The coyote is the wild dog of Mexico's deserts.`},{id:`head-shawl-rebozo`,term:`head shawl (rebozo)`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:1,text:`Juana's long shawl, the rebozo worn by Mexican women for centuries: as a head covering, a sling for a baby and a wrap against the cold.`},{id:`hail-marys-and-ancient-magic`,term:`Hail Marys and ancient magic`,kind:`custom`,chapter:1,text:`Juana prays both Catholic prayers and older charms. Spanish missionaries converted Mexico's Indigenous peoples, but many communities blended the new faith with far older beliefs.`},{id:`the-doctor-s-race`,term:`the doctor's race`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Spain conquered Mexico in the 1500s. Four centuries later, race and class still largely coincided: descendants of the Spanish colonists held wealth and power in town, while Indigenous families like Kino's lived poor on its edges.`},{id:`the-old-language`,term:`the old language`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Kino's people still speak an Indigenous language among themselves; Spanish is the language of the rulers. Answering only in Spanish is a way of putting distance between oneself and one's origins.`},{id:`pearl-diving`,term:`pearl diving`,kind:`custom`,chapter:2,text:`Divers like Kino worked without any breathing gear: a heavy stone took them straight to the bottom, where they filled a basket with oysters on a single breath held for around two minutes, then shot back up.`,placeId:`oyster-bed`},{id:`pearls-and-the-king-of-spain`,term:`pearls and the King of Spain`,kind:`history`,chapter:2,text:`Steinbeck isn't exaggerating: from the 1500s onward, pearls from these Gulf beds were shipped to Spain, adding to the crown's wealth and decorating its churches. Centuries of harvesting left them much depleted.`},{id:`how-a-pearl-forms`,term:`how a pearl forms`,kind:`word`,chapter:2,text:`When a grain of sand or a parasite gets inside an oyster, the oyster coats it in layer upon layer of smooth nacre (mother-of-pearl). Most pearls are small or misshapen; a large, perfect one is extraordinarily rare.`},{id:`poultice`,term:`poultice`,kind:`word`,chapter:2,text:`A soft, moist mass of plant matter pressed onto a wound or swelling to draw out poison or reduce inflammation. Juana's brown seaweed poultice is traditional folk medicine.`},{id:`colonial-animal`,term:`colonial animal`,kind:`reference`,chapter:3,text:`Steinbeck was a keen amateur marine biologist. Some sea creatures, like corals, are colonies of many small organisms joined so closely they act as one body: his image for how a town thinks and feels.`},{id:`church-wedding`,term:`church wedding`,kind:`custom`,chapter:3,text:`Priests charged fees for weddings and baptisms that the poorest families often couldn't afford, so many couples lived together for years without a church marriage. That is why the wedding matters so much to Kino.`},{id:`the-rifle`,term:`the rifle`,kind:`custom`,chapter:3,text:`For a poor fisherman, a rifle was an almost unthinkable possession: a mark of status, independence and power, the kind of thing only better-off men owned.`},{id:`schooling`,term:`schooling`,kind:`history`,chapter:3,text:`In 1940s rural Mexico many poor and Indigenous people never learned to read. Kino understands that literacy is power: someone who can read contracts and do sums is much harder to cheat.`},{id:`alms`,term:`alms`,kind:`word`,chapter:3,text:`Money or food given to the poor as charity. The beggars outside the church live on alms, and a rich man is a generous man, they hope.`},{id:`pesos`,term:`pesos`,kind:`money`,chapter:4,text:`Mexico's currency. In the 1940s a peso was worth roughly 20 US cents, so 1,000 pesos was about $200 then (a few thousand dollars today). Fifty thousand pesos would have been a fortune to a man who usually saw almost no cash.`},{id:`a-single-buyer`,term:`a single buyer`,kind:`custom`,chapter:4,text:`If all the buyers secretly work for one man, they aren't really competing, and the seller has no way to get a fair price. Economists call a market with only one buyer a monopsony.`,placeId:`pearl-buyers-offices`},{id:`the-priest-s-sermon`,term:`the priest's sermon`,kind:`custom`,chapter:4,text:`The townspeople remember a yearly sermon: each person is like a soldier God has placed to guard part of the universe, and must not leave his post. For centuries such teaching urged the poor to accept their place in life.`},{id:`breaking-a-boat`,term:`breaking a boat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:5,text:`For a fisherman the canoe is his living and his family's inheritance. Steinbeck says his people think it worse to kill a boat than a man, since a boat has no sons and cannot protect itself.`},{id:`lament-for-the-dead`,term:`lament for the dead`,kind:`custom`,chapter:5,text:`Traditional mourning in many communities includes ritual wailing for the dead by the women of the family, sung or chanted aloud so that neighbours know and join in.`},{id:`family-duty`,term:`family duty`,kind:`custom`,chapter:5,text:`In Kino's world a brother's claim on shelter and help is nearly absolute, even when giving it puts one's own family in danger.`},{id:`baja-s-mountains`,term:`Baja's mountains`,kind:`place`,chapter:6,text:`Baja California is a long, mostly desert peninsula with rugged, dry mountain ranges running down its spine. Water is scarce, so people and animals alike depend on the few known springs and rock pools.`,placeId:`the-mountains`},{id:`trackers`,term:`trackers`,kind:`custom`,chapter:6,text:`Expert readers of the ground who follow a trail by footprints, overturned stones, broken twigs and crushed grass. Such men were hired to hunt down people or animals.`},{id:`white-clothes`,term:`white clothes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:6,text:`Poor Mexican men of the time typically wore loose white cotton shirts and trousers, cool and cheap, but very visible in the dark.`}],primers:[{chapter:1,setting:`A beach settlement of brush huts beside a small town on the Gulf of California — dawn`,keepInMind:`Steinbeck opens by calling this a story people tell, with only good and bad things in it and no in-between. Read it as a folk tale or parable as much as a realistic story.`,returning:[]},{chapter:2,setting:`The beach and the oyster bed — the same morning`,keepInMind:`A scorpion stung baby Coyotito, and the doctor refused to treat him because Kino could pay only with a few small, poor pearls. The sea is Kino's only source of money.`,returning:[]},{chapter:3,setting:`The town, as news spreads`,keepInMind:`Kino has found an enormous, perfect pearl, and the swelling on Coyotito's shoulder seemed to be going down. Earlier, the doctor turned the family away from his gate.`,returning:[]},{chapter:4,setting:`The town — the morning Kino is to sell his pearl`,keepInMind:`Kino fought off an intruder in the night, and Juana begged him to throw the pearl away. He means to sell it and pay for a church wedding, a rifle and schooling for Coyotito.`,returning:[]},{chapter:5,setting:`Kino's brush house — late that night`,keepInMind:`Kino refused the buyers' low offers and vowed to sell in the capital, despite Juan Tomás's warning. He was attacked again outside the hut, and Juana again pleaded with him to give the pearl up.`,returning:[{id:`apolonia`,reminder:`Juan Tomás's wife, Kino's sister-in-law, a neighbour in the brush houses.`}]},{chapter:6,setting:`Heading north from the town, by night`,keepInMind:`Kino killed a man who attacked him; his canoe was smashed and his house burned. After hiding a day with Juan Tomás, the family has set out on foot toward the capital, still carrying the pearl.`,returning:[]}],recaps:[{through:3,title:`The greatest pearl`,text:[`Kino, a poor pearl diver, lives in a brush hut by the sea with his wife, Juana, and their baby son, Coyotito. One peaceful dawn a scorpion stings Coyotito. The family carries him to the town doctor, but the doctor refuses to treat a poor native family who cannot pay.`,`While Juana tends the baby with a seaweed poultice and prays for luck, Kino dives for oysters and brings up a huge one. Inside lies the greatest pearl anyone has ever seen.`,`Before Kino is even home, the whole town knows. The priest, the shopkeepers, the beggars and the pearl buyers all begin to want something from the pearl. Kino tells his neighbours and his brother, Juan Tomás, what it will buy: a church wedding, new clothes, a rifle, and above all schooling for Coyotito. Suddenly the doctor comes to treat the baby after all, gives him a white powder that makes him violently ill, then “cures” him, and Kino suspects a trick.`,`As chapter 3 closes, someone has crept into the hut in the night to steal the pearl, and Kino has fought him off in the dark and been struck on the head. Juana begs him to throw the pearl back into the sea, saying it will destroy them, but Kino refuses: this is their one chance, and he means to fight for it.`]},{through:5,title:`Flight into the night`,text:[`When a scorpion stung his baby son, Coyotito, the poor pearl diver Kino was turned away by the town doctor. Then Kino found the greatest pearl anyone had ever seen, and dreamed of a church wedding with his wife, Juana, a rifle and schooling for his son. But the pearl set the whole town against him. The doctor suddenly came to “treat” the baby, and that night an intruder tried to steal the pearl.`,`Kino took the pearl to the buyers, who pretended to compete but each offered only a fraction of its worth. Sensing a trap, he refused and vowed to sell it in the capital, despite his brother Juan Tomás’s warning that men who tried that were never heard from again. That night he was attacked once more.`,`In the dark, Juana tried to throw the pearl into the sea, and Kino caught her and beat her. On the way back he was attacked again and killed a man. Then the family found their canoe smashed and their hut in flames. They hid all day in Juan Tomás’s house, while the neighbours thought them dead, and Kino told his brother that the pearl has become his soul.`,`As chapter 5 closes, darkness has fallen, and Kino, Juana and Coyotito have set out on foot, heading north toward the capital with the pearl.`]}],wordCounts:[4090,2830,5670,4720,4090,8190],atlas:{frames:[{id:`gulf`,type:`geo`,title:`The Gulf & the mountains`,short:`The Gulf`,bounds:[[-111.05,23.85],[-109.75,24.95]],note:`Steinbeck never names the town; it is modelled on La Paz, Baja California Sur. Inland places are approximate.`,labels:[{text:`Gulf of California`,at:[-110,24.62],kind:`sea`},{text:`Bay of La Paz`,at:[-110.47,24.33],kind:`sea`},{text:`Sierra de la Giganta`,at:[-110.82,24.78],kind:`area`}]},{id:`town`,type:`plan`,title:`The town & the beach`,short:`The town`,parent:`gulf`,anchor:[-110.31,24.155],size:[1e3,700],note:`Drawn from the book’s descriptions, not to scale.`,place:`the-town`}],places:[{id:`doctors-house`,name:`The doctor’s house`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:1,precision:`fictional`,lonlat:[-110.309,24.156],radiusKm:1,xy:{town:[690,250]},parent:`the-town`,tagline:[{from:1,text:`The doctor’s house in town, behind a wall with an iron gate, where Kino is turned away.`}]},{id:`kinos-house`,name:`Kino’s brush house`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:1,precision:`fictional`,lonlat:[-110.316,24.158],radiusKm:1,xy:{town:[380,470]},parent:`the-town`,note:`brush house`,tagline:[{from:1,text:`The family’s small brush hut near the beach.`},{from:5,text:`The family’s hut, burned to the ground.`}]},{id:`the-church`,name:`The church`,role:`mentioned`,firstChapter:1,precision:`fictional`,lonlat:[-110.311,24.158],radiusKm:1,xy:{town:[520,170]},parent:`the-town`,tagline:[{from:1,text:`The town church, with beggars on its steps.`},{from:3,text:`The town church, whose priest hopes the pearl will pay for repairs.`}]},{id:`the-town`,name:`The town`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:1,precision:`approximate`,lonlat:[-110.31,24.155],radiusKm:2,xy:{town:[500,230]},today:`La Paz, capital of Baja California Sur, on the Bahía de La Paz.`,note:`La Paz`,tagline:[{from:1,text:`The unnamed seaside town of stone and plaster, above the brush huts of Kino's people.`},{from:4,text:`The seaside town where everyone wants a share of Kino's pearl.`}]},{id:`oyster-bed`,name:`The oyster bed`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:2,precision:`approximate`,lonlat:[-110.35,24.23],radiusKm:5,parent:`the-gulf`,note:`pearl diving`,tagline:[{from:2,text:`The oyster bed out in the gulf, where Kino finds the great pearl.`}]},{id:`the-beach`,name:`The beach`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:2,precision:`fictional`,lonlat:[-110.317,24.161],radiusKm:1,xy:{town:[300,545]},parent:`the-town`,tagline:[{from:2,text:`The shore below the brush houses, where the canoes are drawn up.`},{from:5,text:`The shore where Kino’s canoe lay, now holed and ruined.`},{from:6,text:`The water’s edge where Kino throws the pearl back into the sea.`}]},{id:`the-estuary`,name:`The estuary`,role:`mentioned`,firstChapter:2,precision:`fictional`,xy:{town:[870,520]},parent:`the-town`,tagline:[{from:2,text:`The broad, mangrove-fringed estuary the town lies on.`}]},{id:`the-gulf`,name:`The Gulf`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:2,precision:`approximate`,lonlat:[-110.5,24.4],radiusKm:25,xy:{town:[450,640]},today:`The Bahía de La Paz, an arm of the Gulf of California (Sea of Cortez).`,tagline:[{from:2,text:`The sea where Kino dives for oysters.`},{from:6,text:`The sea that takes the pearl back.`}],aliases:[{name:`the sea`,from:2}]},{id:`pearl-buyers-offices`,name:`The pearl buyers’ street`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:3,precision:`fictional`,lonlat:[-110.313,24.157],radiusKm:1,xy:{town:[400,300]},parent:`the-town`,note:`a single buyer`,tagline:[{from:3,text:`The street of little offices where the town’s pearl buyers sit.`},{from:4,text:`The street of pearl buyers’ offices, where Kino is offered a fraction of the pearl’s worth.`}]},{id:`the-capital`,name:`The capital`,role:`mentioned`,firstChapter:4,precision:`unknown`,tagline:[{from:4,text:`The distant city where Kino hopes to sell his pearl.`}]},{id:`juan-tomas-house`,name:`Juan Tomás’s house`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:5,precision:`fictional`,lonlat:[-110.3155,24.1585],radiusKm:1,xy:{town:[450,465]},parent:`the-town`,tagline:[{from:5,text:`The brush house of Kino’s brother, where the family hides.`}]},{id:`the-mountain-pool`,name:`The mountain pool`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:6,precision:`approximate`,lonlat:[-110.86,24.45],radiusKm:15,parent:`the-mountains`,tagline:[{from:6,text:`A pool of water high in the rocks, with caves above it.`}]},{id:`the-mountains`,name:`The mountains`,role:`setting`,firstChapter:6,precision:`approximate`,lonlat:[-110.82,24.5],radiusKm:30,today:`Dry ranges north-west of La Paz, at the southern end of the Sierra de la Giganta.`,note:`Baja's mountains`,tagline:[{from:6,text:`Dry, rocky mountains north of the town, where Kino and Juana flee from the trackers.`}]}],settings:{1:[`kinos-house`,`the-town`,`doctors-house`],2:[`oyster-bed`,`the-beach`,`the-gulf`],3:[`kinos-house`],4:[`pearl-buyers-offices`,`kinos-house`,`the-town`],5:[`kinos-house`,`the-beach`,`juan-tomas-house`],6:[`the-mountain-pool`,`the-mountains`,`the-town`,`the-beach`,`the-gulf`]},journeys:[{id:`kino-dives`,name:`Kino’s canoe to the oyster bed`,who:[`kino`,`juana`,`coyotito`],legs:[{chapter:2,from:`the-beach`,to:`oyster-bed`,mode:`sea`,via:[[-110.322,24.195]]}]},{id:`flight-north`,name:`The flight north`,who:[`kino`,`juana`,`coyotito`],legs:[{chapter:6,from:`the-town`,to:`the-mountain-pool`,mode:`foot`,via:[[-110.413,24.102],[-110.7,24.28]]}]},{id:`trackers-pursuit`,name:`The trackers’ pursuit`,who:[`the-trackers`],legs:[{chapter:6,from:`the-town`,to:`the-mountain-pool`,mode:`foot`,via:[[-110.413,24.102],[-110.7,24.28]]}]},{id:`return-to-town`,name:`The return to the town`,who:[`kino`,`juana`],legs:[{chapter:6,from:`the-mountain-pool`,to:`the-town`,mode:`foot`,via:[[-110.7,24.28],[-110.413,24.102]]},{chapter:6,from:`the-town`,to:`the-beach`,mode:`foot`}]}]},themes:[{id:`good-evil`,name:`Good and evil`,from:1,summary:[{from:1,text:`The Songs divide Kino's world into the Family and Evil, and a scorpion brings evil into a perfect morning; the story is framed as a parable with nothing in between.`},{from:2,text:`A pearl begins as a grain of sand that wounds an oyster; the great pearl arrives as luck, with no clear sign of whether it is a blessing.`},{from:3,text:`Kino's plans set off a struggle in the dark: an intruder, a wound, and Juana's conviction that the pearl is evil.`},{from:5,text:`Kino beats Juana and kills a man; good and evil are no longer outside him but in his own hands.`},{from:6,text:`In the hills Kino fights as the Songs of the Family and the Enemy clash, and he is forced to weigh what the pearl has cost.`}]},{id:`family`,name:`Family and the songs`,from:1,summary:[{from:1,text:`The Song of the Family means wholeness: Kino, Juana and Coyotito, a fire, corncakes. Juana is the one who acts when Coyotito is stung.`},{from:2,text:`Juana treats the baby with what she knows and prays for Kino's luck; the canoe, an heirloom, keeps the family alive.`},{from:3,text:`Kino's dreams are for the family, above all school for Coyotito; Juana begins to fear the pearl will destroy them.`},{from:4,text:`Kino's manhood and Juana's fear pull against each other, while Juan Tomás warns his brother with love.`},{from:5,text:`Kino strikes Juana, yet she chooses to stay with him; Juan Tomás and Apolonia shelter them at great risk.`},{from:6,text:`On the run, the family's song turns fierce and protective, and everything rests on keeping Coyotito safe.`}]},{id:`colonial-power`,name:`Power and the poor`,from:1,summary:[{from:1,text:`The doctor, from the race that conquered Kino's people, refuses to treat a poor Indigenous baby; the servant will not speak his own language.`},{from:2,text:`Juana's free remedy is worth nothing in a world that values only what is paid for.`},{from:3,text:`Every powerful figure in town, priest and doctor included, plans to profit from Kino's pearl.`},{from:4,text:`The buyers' rigged market and the priest's sermon about staying in one's place show a whole system set against Kino.`},{from:6,text:`Hired trackers and a rifleman hunt the family across the hills.`}]},{id:`nature`,name:`Nature and survival`,from:2,summary:[{from:2,text:`The Gulf's haze makes seeing uncertain, and a pearl is the sea's accident, a grain of sand turned treasure.`},{from:3,text:`In the estuary small fish flee great fish, and night hawks hunt mice, a quiet mirror of the town.`},{from:5,text:`After the killing Kino becomes like an animal, living only to hide and survive.`},{from:6,text:`Across a dry, thorny land every creature hunts or hides; the mountain pool draws life, and so draws killers.`}]},{id:`greed`,name:`Greed and corruption`,from:3,summary:[{from:3,text:`News of the pearl reveals what everyone already wanted; the doctor treats the baby only now there may be payment.`},{from:4,text:`The pearl buyers fake a competition to cheat Kino of the pearl's worth.`},{from:5,text:`The violence spreads: a canoe smashed, a house burned, a man dead, and Kino says the pearl has become his soul.`},{from:6,text:`Men are sent to hunt the family down for the pearl, and Kino sees it at last for what it has brought them.`}]},{id:`dreams`,name:`Dreams and ambition`,from:3,summary:[{from:3,text:`Kino sees his future in the pearl: a church wedding, clothes, a rifle, and schooling for Coyotito.`},{from:4,text:`Refusing the buyers, Kino stakes everything on selling the pearl in the capital.`},{from:5,text:`Kino clings to the dream even as it costs him his boat and his home.`},{from:6,text:`Hope flickers again on the road north, but the pictures in the pearl begin to darken.`}]}],insights:[{id:`1-1`,chapter:1,kind:`craft`,title:`A story told as a parable`,text:`Before the story starts, Steinbeck says this is a tale the town retells, a world of good and bad with nothing in between, and that each reader may take their own meaning from it. That framing explains a lot. Most people have no names, only roles: the doctor, the servant, the beggars. Read it as a fable as much as a novel, and ask what each figure stands for.`,themes:[`good-evil`]},{id:`1-2`,chapter:1,kind:`motif`,title:`Music in Kino's head`,text:`Kino hears songs instead of thinking in words. The Song of the Family is calm and whole; the Song of Evil comes in with the scorpion. Steinbeck uses them as a gauge of danger and safety. Notice how quickly one song can drown out the other.`,themes:[`family`,`good-evil`]},{id:`1-3`,chapter:1,kind:`motif`,title:`The ant and the scorpion`,text:`Early that morning Kino idly watches an ant struggling to escape an ant lion's sand trap, and he does nothing, as detached as a god. Minutes later a small creature threatens his own child. Steinbeck sets the family inside a world of hunters and hunted from the first page. Who in the town plays the ant lion?`,themes:[`good-evil`]},{id:`1-4`,chapter:1,kind:`theme`,title:`Two worlds at the doctor's gate`,text:`The walk into town crosses a line: from brush huts to stone walls, from Kino's language to Spanish. The doctor is drawn through his comforts, his chocolate and his memories of Paris, and he speaks of the family as if they were animals. Kino's feelings mix fear, anger and an old habit of deference, and they are four centuries old.`,themes:[`colonial-power`]},{id:`1-5`,chapter:1,kind:`theme`,title:`Juana's strength`,text:`It is Juana, not Kino, who sucks the poison from the wound and who demands the doctor. Steinbeck shows her mixing Hail Marys with older charms, and acting while everyone else stares.`,themes:[`family`]},{id:`2-1`,chapter:2,kind:`motif`,title:`The uncertain air of the Gulf`,text:`Steinbeck opens with a haze over the water that makes distant things look near and near things blur, so that no one can trust what they see. It is a real effect of the Gulf, but he says it also shapes how people imagine. A chapter about finding something wonderful begins by warning us about seeing clearly.`,themes:[`nature`]},{id:`2-2`,chapter:2,kind:`theme`,title:`The remedy that costs nothing`,text:`Juana's seaweed poultice may work as well as anything the doctor could offer, the narrator says, but no one values it because it is free. That one line turns the doctor's refusal from the last chapter into a comment on how worth is measured. Knowledge counts only when it is paid for and comes from the right people.`,themes:[`colonial-power`,`family`]},{id:`2-3`,chapter:2,kind:`motif`,title:`The Song of the Pearl That Might Be`,text:`Under the water Kino hears a new, secret song, a melody of hope rather than of home. Note the name: it is a song of possibility, not of anything he has. It is the first song in the book about wanting.`,themes:[`family`]},{id:`2-4`,chapter:2,kind:`craft`,title:`Slowing down before the opening`,text:`Kino does not open the big oyster first. He opens the small ones, and is reluctant to want anything too openly in case it drives luck away. Steinbeck stretches the moment so the reveal lands, and he lets us feel the family's superstitious caution about hope itself.`,themes:[`good-evil`]},{id:`2-5`,chapter:2,kind:`context`,title:`A pearl is an accident`,text:`Steinbeck explains that a pearl forms when a grain of sand irritates an oyster and it coats the intruder in layers, over and over. The most prized thing in the Gulf begins as a wound. He also says the divers' whole trade depends on chance, on God or the gods placing a pearl here and not there.`,themes:[`nature`]},{id:`3-1`,chapter:3,kind:`theme`,title:`A town wakes up wanting`,text:`Steinbeck calls the town a living creature with a nervous system, and the news runs through it. Then he goes round the town person by person: priest, shopkeepers, beggars, buyers, doctor. Each sees the pearl as the answer to his own wish, and none of them thinks of Kino. The pearl has not made anyone greedy; it has shown what was already there.`,themes:[`greed`,`colonial-power`]},{id:`3-2`,chapter:3,kind:`motif`,title:`Pictures in the pearl`,text:`When Kino looks into the pearl, he sees his plans on its surface: the wedding, the clothes, the rifle, Coyotito at a desk. The pearl does not show the future. It shows Kino what he wants, and it is the clearest view we get of his mind.`,themes:[`dreams`]},{id:`3-3`,chapter:3,kind:`question`,title:`Why does the rifle matter so much?`,text:`Kino's list moves from things his people might hope for, a church wedding and new clothes, to a rifle, and then to schooling. Steinbeck says that once he has named the rifle, every barrier seems to fall. What does each item mean to him, and what changes in him as he says them out loud?`,themes:[`dreams`,`colonial-power`]},{id:`3-4`,chapter:3,kind:`motif`,title:`Small fish and great fish`,text:`In the middle of the evening Steinbeck turns away to the estuary, where a school of small fish flees larger ones, and to night hawks hunting mice. These quiet passages are never just scenery. Ask who in the human story is the hunter and who the prey.`,themes:[`nature`,`greed`]},{id:`3-5`,chapter:3,kind:`craft`,title:`Darkness enters the house`,text:`The pearl glows with gathered light, but this chapter ends in a dark hut with an unseen intruder. Watch how Steinbeck handles light: candle and firelight for family, blackness for the threat. Juana's answer is to want the pearl gone; Kino's is to fight.`,themes:[`good-evil`,`family`]},{id:`4-1`,chapter:4,kind:`theme`,title:`A market that is a stage`,text:`The buyers act out a competition, one after another, but the town suspects a single man owns them all. Kino cannot prove the trick, but he can feel it. Steinbeck shows a system that cheats the poor without anyone breaking a rule.`,themes:[`colonial-power`,`greed`]},{id:`4-2`,chapter:4,kind:`motif`,title:`The coin on the knuckles`,text:`The first buyer rolls a coin back and forth across his knuckles while he talks. It is a show of ease and control, a small sleight of hand. Notice what his hand does as the bargaining turns against him.`,themes:[`greed`]},{id:`4-3`,chapter:4,kind:`context`,title:`Stay in your place`,text:`Juan Tomás recalls the priest's yearly sermon: each person is like a soldier given a post in a castle, and leaving it is a sin. Seen next to the buyers' deal, the sermon reads as one more piece of the same structure. Juan Tomás warns that Kino has defied not just the buyers but a whole way of life.`,themes:[`colonial-power`]},{id:`4-4`,chapter:4,kind:`question`,title:`What does Kino mean by 'I am a man'?`,text:`Kino answers Juana's fear with his manhood. Is it courage, pride, or something forced on him by the town? Weigh his defiance of the buyers against what it is costing the family, and ask whether Juana sees something he cannot.`,themes:[`dreams`,`family`]},{id:`4-5`,chapter:4,kind:`theme`,title:`The neighbours' chorus`,text:`The whole neighbourhood follows Kino to the buyers and then talks it over afterwards, half admiring and half afraid. Like a chorus in a Greek play, they voice what the community thinks. Their mixed feelings show how dangerous it is for one of them to step out of line.`,themes:[`colonial-power`,`dreams`]},{id:`5-1`,chapter:5,kind:`theme`,title:`Juana sees him clearly`,text:`After Kino beats her, the chapter stays with Juana's mind. She feels no anger. She knows men are part mad and part god, and she decides to follow him anyway. It is a hard passage: Steinbeck gives her understanding and endurance, but asks us to accept her obedience too.`,themes:[`family`,`good-evil`]},{id:`5-2`,chapter:5,kind:`motif`,title:`Kino becomes an animal`,text:`After the killing Steinbeck says Kino is now like an animal, living only to hide, attack and protect his own. In the first chapter he watched the hunters and the hunted from above. Now he is one of the hunted.`,themes:[`nature`,`good-evil`]},{id:`5-3`,chapter:5,kind:`craft`,title:`Losing everything in one night`,text:`Canoe, house and safety vanish in quick succession, one blow after another. Steinbeck keeps the sentences plain and the pace fast, and lets the canoe hurt most: it was his grandfather's, and his living. Ask what Kino still owns by the end of the night.`,themes:[`greed`,`family`]},{id:`5-4`,chapter:5,kind:`question`,title:`Who owns whom?`,text:`Kino tells his brother that the pearl has become his soul and that to give it up would be to lose himself. In chapter 3 it held his hopes for his family. What has changed, and is Kino still choosing, or has the pearl taken hold of him?`,themes:[`greed`,`dreams`]},{id:`5-5`,chapter:5,kind:`theme`,title:`Juan Tomás and Apolonia`,text:`Hiding a man who has killed puts Juan Tomás at real risk, yet he does it without hesitation and lies for them. Apolonia's mourning for the family she believes dead shows how seriously the community grieves. Against the town's greed, the bond between brothers holds.`,themes:[`family`]},{id:`6-1`,chapter:6,kind:`motif`,title:`Hunters like hounds`,text:`The trackers bend over the ground like hunting dogs, reading signs Kino cannot hide. The whole chapter is a chase across a dry land full of creatures that kill or flee. Steinbeck notes that the mountain pool draws life with its water, and so draws killers too.`,themes:[`nature`,`colonial-power`]},{id:`6-2`,chapter:6,kind:`motif`,title:`The songs turn fierce`,text:`In the hills the Song of the Family returns, but it is no longer peaceful. It becomes fierce and cold, like the snarl of a wild animal guarding its young, and the Song of the Enemy is loud in Kino's ears. Listen to how the songs sound now compared with that first quiet morning.`,themes:[`family`,`good-evil`]},{id:`6-3`,chapter:6,kind:`craft`,title:`Light and dark in the final night`,text:`Kino strips off his white clothes to become part of the dark, while the moon rises and threatens to expose him. Steinbeck has used light and dark all along; here they become a matter of life and death. Notice how a sound, not a sight, decides everything.`,themes:[`good-evil`]},{id:`6-4`,chapter:6,kind:`motif`,title:`The last look into the pearl`,text:`Watch what Kino sees in the pearl now compared with chapter 3. The bright pictures of his future turn into images of violence and loss, and the pearl itself looks ugly to him. It was always a mirror of his mind.`,themes:[`dreams`,`greed`]},{id:`6-5`,chapter:6,kind:`craft`,title:`Side by side`,text:`Kino and Juana walk back into town side by side, not with Juana behind him. The town watches in silence. At the shore Kino offers the pearl to Juana first, and she gives the choice back to him. Steinbeck tells this in a slow, ceremonial rhythm, as a legend the town will retell.`,themes:[`family`,`good-evil`]}],afterword:JSON.parse(`{"ending":["The story ends where it began, at the edge of the sea, but everything in between has been lost. In the mountains Kino attacks the trackers before dawn. Coyotito cries out in the cave, a tracker takes the sound for a coyote pup, and the rifleman fires at it. Kino kills all three men, but the shot has already killed his son. The baby whose sting started the story, and whose schooling was the heart of Kino's dream, is the price the pearl finally takes.","Kino and Juana walk back into town in the late afternoon. Steinbeck tells it as a legend: the town remembers them as if they had passed beyond ordinary life, carrying the rifle and the small bundle in the shawl. Usually Juana walks a little behind Kino. Now they walk side by side, as equals in grief. Whatever happened between them on the beach, the ending treats them as partners who have suffered the same thing.","At the shore Kino looks into the pearl one last time. The bright pictures of chapter 3 are gone; he sees the dead man, Juana beaten, his child. The pearl has turned grey and ugly, because it always reflected what was in his mind. He offers it to Juana, who once tried to throw it away herself, and she gives the act back to him. He throws it as far as he can, and it sinks into the green water, the Song of the Pearl fading to a whisper.","Why does it land so hard? Because Kino gets nothing back. He keeps no money, no canoe, no house, and no son. Throwing the pearl away is not a reward; it is a refusal to let it own them any longer. The family is free of the thing that destroyed them, but they return to a world that is just as unjust as before. A parable usually gives you a clear moral. This one leaves you holding the cost."],"themes":[{"theme":"good-evil","text":"The story's frame promises a world of black and white, but by the end good and evil are tangled inside Kino himself. He is a loving father who beats his wife and kills four men. Steinbeck's evil comes from outside, in the doctor, the buyers and the trackers, but it works by pulling the worst out of a good man. Throwing back the pearl is less a victory of good than an act of clearing away."},{"theme":"family","text":"The Song of the Family is the book's measure of what matters, and the pearl breaks it piece by piece: Juana beaten, the home burned, Coyotito killed. Yet the family holds in another sense. Juan Tomás risks everything to hide them, and Kino and Juana come home side by side. The last decision about the pearl is shared between husband and wife."},{"theme":"colonial-power","text":"Kino's real enemy is never one person. It is the whole order the priest preaches about, in which the descendants of conquerors set prices and decide who receives medicine. When Kino tries to step out of his place, the system answers with lies, then fire, then hired guns. The ending does not overturn that order; it shows what it costs to defy it."},{"theme":"nature","text":"The ant and the ant lion, the small fish and the great, the creatures at the mountain pool: Steinbeck's estuary and desert are full of hunters and prey, and humans take their place among them. Kino moves from watching this world like a god to being hunted through it like an animal. The final image returns the pearl to the sea that made it, as if putting something back in its proper place."},{"theme":"greed","text":"The pearl awakens greed in the town at once, and in the end it hollows out Kino too, until he calls it his soul. Steinbeck is careful: the town's greed is the more vicious kind, but Kino's longing is also a hunger that grows. The only way to end the corruption is to give up the pearl entirely."},{"theme":"dreams","text":"Kino's dreams were good ones, especially the hope that Coyotito would read and so be free. The pearl showed him that future, then took the boy it was for. The book asks whether the dream was wrong, or whether a world rigged against the poor simply made it impossible. It leaves Kino's hope neither mocked nor rewarded, only crushed."}],"interpretations":[{"title":"Parable or realistic novel?","text":"Steinbeck calls the book a parable and invites each reader to find their own meaning. Flat, nameless types like the doctor and the priest, the Songs, and the folk-tale ending all support that reading. But the poverty, the rigged market and the racial order are drawn from real life in 1940s Mexico, and the violence feels real rather than symbolic. Much of the book's power lies in that tension: a fable that refuses to stay safely a fable."},{"title":"A Marxist or colonial reading","text":"Many readers see the book as an attack on economic and colonial exploitation. The buyers' monopoly, the doctor's contempt, and the priest's sermon about staying in one's station form a single structure that keeps Indigenous people poor. On this reading the pearl is capital itself: it promises freedom, but the system that controls its value ensures it destroys the worker who found it. Others caution that Steinbeck offers no political remedy, only a family's withdrawal."},{"title":"Is Kino a tragic hero?","text":"Kino has some marks of a tragic hero: a noble aim, a fatal pride in his manhood, a great fall and a final moment of recognition. But tragedy usually depends on a flaw of the hero's own making, and much of what crushes Kino is simply the injustice around him. Is the book about a man's mistake, or about a world that leaves a poor man no right choice? Readers still argue about how much Kino could have done differently."},{"title":"Juana and gender","text":"Juana is often the wisest person in the book. She sees the danger of the pearl first, acts when Coyotito is stung, and tries to throw it away. Yet Steinbeck has her accept Kino's violence calmly and follow him out of loyalty, and her thoughts about what men are can read as an endorsement of that order. Some readers see her as the book's moral centre; others see a woman whose insight the story admires but never fully empowers. The side-by-side walk and the shared final decision suggest a change."},{"title":"A story shaped for film","text":"Steinbeck wrote The Pearl knowing it would become a Mexican film, and he worked on the screenplay at the same time. That may explain its visual, scene-by-scene structure, its few locations, its sound cues in the Songs, and its stark images of light and dark. Some critics think this made it cinematic and timeless; others think it made the characters thinner and more symbolic than in his best novels."}],"context":[{"title":"The Sea of Cortez and the La Paz tale","text":"In 1940 Steinbeck sailed around the Gulf of California with his friend the marine biologist Ed Ricketts, collecting specimens. In their book Sea of Cortez (1941) he retells a story he heard in La Paz: a poor Indian boy finds an enormous pearl, is cheated by the buyers and attacked, and finally throws it back into the sea, free again. Steinbeck saw it as almost too perfect, like a parable. The Pearl expands that bare tale, adding a wife and a child, which makes the cost far heavier."},{"title":"An ecologist's eye","text":"Ricketts taught Steinbeck to look at life as a web of relations, tide pools where every creature eats or is eaten. That habit runs through the book's estuary and desert passages, which set the human story inside a larger struggle for survival. Steinbeck often wrote about people as members of groups, and the town as a single living animal is a direct expression of that idea."},{"title":"Publication, 1945 to 1947","text":"The story first appeared as The Pearl of the World in a women's magazine, Woman's Home Companion, in December 1945. It came out as a book in 1947, with illustrations by the Mexican muralist José Clemente Orozco, the same year the film was released. Coming after the war, it spoke to readers anxious about money, power and the dream of sudden wealth."},{"title":"Mexico in the 1940s","text":"Centuries after the Spanish conquest, race and class in Mexico still overlapped. Indigenous communities like Kino's often lived poorly at the edges of towns run by people of Spanish descent, and pearl fishing in the Gulf had been dominated by outside buyers since colonial times. By the 1940s the Gulf's pearl beds had largely collapsed from overharvesting and disease, so Steinbeck's story already looked back at a vanishing trade."}],"adaptations":[{"title":"La Perla","year":1947,"kind":"film","note":"Mexican film directed by Emilio Fernández and shot by Gabriel Figueroa, co-written by Steinbeck; made in Spanish and English versions."},{"title":"The Pearl","year":2001,"kind":"film","note":"Low-budget English-language version directed by Alfredo Zacarías, with Richard Harris; released only years later."}],"readNext":[{"title":"Of Mice and Men","author":"John Steinbeck","why":"Another short, tightly built Steinbeck story about poor people whose dream of a better life is crushed."},{"title":"The Log from the Sea of Cortez","author":"John Steinbeck","why":"The account of the 1940 voyage, where you can read the original La Paz pearl tale."},{"title":"The Old Man and the Sea","author":"Ernest Hemingway","why":"A short parable of a poor fisherman, a great prize and the sea that takes it back."},{"title":"Pedro Páramo","author":"Juan Rulfo","why":"A haunting Mexican novel of rural poverty and the power of one man over a whole town."},{"title":"Things Fall Apart","author":"Chinua Achebe","why":"A strong man destroyed as a colonial order closes in on his community."},{"title":"Cannery Row","author":"John Steinbeck","why":"A warmer side of Steinbeck, with a hero based on Ed Ricketts."}],"questions":["Steinbeck says each reader takes their own meaning from this parable. What meaning did you take, and did it change by the last page?","Is the pearl itself evil, or does it only reveal what is already in people? Use the doctor, the buyers and Kino as evidence.","Could Kino have made different choices, or did the system around him leave him none?","How do you respond to Juana's acceptance of Kino's violence? Is she the book's moral centre, its victim, or both?","Why does Kino offer the pearl to Juana before he throws it away, and why does she refuse it?","What do the animal images, from the scorpion and ants to the trackers, add to the human story?","Is the ending hopeful in any way, or only tragic? What does it mean that they walk side by side?","Kino's dream of schooling for Coyotito was a good one. What is the book saying about hope for people born poor?"]}`)};export{t as default};